WEBVTT

00:00:00.649 --> 00:00:22.193
<v Riley>Hey there, good to have you. It's Tuesday, so we're in the Agriculture and Ranching stretch. Today we've got Osiris eyeing the Miracle copper-gold property east of Williams Lake, and Clinton's community forest salvaging wood after the Pear Lake fire. Plus Poland's contract fight and whether a family with no savings can ever refuse a bad deal. Stay with us.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:55.644
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Tuesday, November 24th, 2026. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:56.644 --> 00:01:24.332
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Mines, forests, cattle, and a community forest still counting its losses, and the people who stay with all of it. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast and around 2 degrees, with a high of 5 and a low of 1 today and a 24 percent chance of precipitation. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 1 under overcast, Williams Lake around 3 under overcast, Quesnel around 4 with light drizzle, and Riske Creek around 0 under overcast.

00:01:24.532 --> 00:01:25.388
<v Riley>On to the news.

00:01:26.788 --> 00:02:13.982
<v Casey>Most of today's news is about who works the land and who gets paid for it, and a lot of it lands close to home. Osiris Copper Corp. has agreed to acquire an option on the Miracle Copper-Gold Project, a 6,300-hectare property about 72 kilometres east of Williams Lake. The company's statement came out on November 16th. The property sits immediately west of Imperial Metals' Mount Polley mine, and about 34 and a half kilometres east of the Gibraltar mine. Two copper-gold targets, the Miracle Porphyry and the Trio Lake Porphyry, have had historical exploration but very little systematic work. Osiris CEO Sreenath Didugu calls it an attractive opportunity in one of B.C.'s most established copper districts. The company has completed airborne magnetic and electromagnetic surveys so far.

00:02:13.982 --> 00:02:17.622
<v Riley>An option is the right to explore first. Nothing has been mined.

00:02:19.413 --> 00:02:59.011
<v Riley>B.C.'s Labour Relations Board has partly sided with the United Steelworkers Local 7619 in its dispute with Teck's Highland Valley Copper mine near Logan Lake. Board vice-chair Paul Todd released the decision on Friday, November 13th, after a four-day hearing. The union says the company used replacement workers and contractors who aren't eligible to do the work of employees on strike. The union started with seven instances and narrowed them to five. The headline outcome is an order to stop using ineligible replacement workers, and the ruling is partial. The union represents 1,200 employees at the mine.

00:02:59.461 --> 00:03:04.133
<v Casey>Highland Valley is the largest open-pit copper mine in Canada, so this is no small dispute.

00:03:05.893 --> 00:03:40.403
<v Casey>Between 60 and 70 per cent of the Clinton Community Forest burned in the Pear Lake fire this summer. The forest covers about 62,000 hectares, and general manager Steve Law says roughly 40,000 of those hectares burned. Not all of that timber is merchantable, and not all of it can be reached, so salvage won't cover it all. The fire forced the evacuation of Clinton on July 24th, and the town itself was largely preserved. The forest was incorporated in 2013 as an area-based tenure in the 100 Mile Timber Supply Area. Salvage efforts are underway.

00:03:40.852 --> 00:03:44.988
<v Riley>Burned timber loses value while it waits, so salvage runs on a clock.

00:03:46.677 --> 00:04:17.853
<v Riley>The BC Wildfire Service is conducting a pile burn near the 99 Mile Bike Trails, about five kilometres southwest of 100 Mile House. The project is a partnership with South Area Parks and Recreation. The stated goal is to reduce wildfire risk to 100 Mile House residences and surrounding areas by removing forest fuels, which cuts both how far a fire can spread and how hot it burns. The BC Wildfire Service also asks people to report unattended campfires and open-burning violations.

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:42.938
<v Casey>Hundreds of people marked Remembrance Day on November 11th at ceremonies in 100 Mile House, Lac La Hache, and Forest Grove. The Prime Minister's proclamation marked the hundredth anniversary of the Royal Canadian Legion. At the 100 Mile House ceremony, district councillor Donna Barnett read a statement from Cariboo-Chilcotin MLA Lorne Doerkson, who was unable to attend any of the South Cariboo ceremonies.

00:04:44.351 --> 00:05:16.805
<v Riley>Canada's own projections showed a best case of a 28 per cent cut in emissions from 2005 levels by 2030. The Paris commitment is 40 to 45 per cent. Since those projections, the government has rolled back several climate policies, and in June Prime Minister Mark Carney said emissions would be higher in the next few years than projected. The Castanet piece argues Ottawa won't say outright that the target is out of reach. The numbers in question come from the government's own emissions progress report.

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:32.597
<v Casey>We covered the Tumbler Ridge shooter's ChatGPT logs on Sunday. A new report by Mark Follman at Mother Jones adds an allegation: the chatbot helped her test and troubleshoot a magazine she had made with a 3D printer, ahead of the February 10th attack.

00:05:34.144 --> 00:05:54.544
<v Riley>Vancouver Sun columnist Douglas Todd looks at the big, remote ridings the B.C. Conservatives won, where loggers, miners, and oil-and-gas workers make up much of the electorate. He asks what that means for the resource industry, health care, and housing. The column's argument is that Interior residents feel their time has finally come.

00:05:54.994 --> 00:06:00.202
<v Casey>Those ridings run on the resource industry, and Todd's column puts it first on the list for a reason.

00:06:01.878 --> 00:06:17.478
<v Casey>The Tyee looks at Logan Lake, the town built around the Highland Valley operation, which it calls an instant town. The strike in that Labour Board ruling is what's shaking it. A journalist wrote in 1977 that in Logan Lake, it's all copper. The Tyee says not much has changed.

00:06:17.928 --> 00:06:21.736
<v Riley>One mine, one town, and one employer on the other side of the table.

00:06:23.414 --> 00:06:56.476
<v Riley>Brazil's National Council for Industrialization of Critical and Strategic Minerals approved a list of 27 critical minerals on November 5th. The minerals are sorted into three priority groups based on national security, industrial development, and supply chain resilience. The list determines which projects qualify for support from Brazil's federal government. Brazil's minister of development, industry, commerce and services says the country has long exported mineral wealth as ore and imported it back as finished product.

00:06:57.894 --> 00:07:20.814
<v Casey>A first-day dispatch from Islamabad argues Pakistan's mineral race turns on transport routes as much as on what sits underground. The writer flew over Balochistan, where the Reko Diq and Saindak deposits are, and over Gwadar, the deep-water port China has spent more than a decade building. The argument is that roads, rails, and harbours decide who gets the minerals out. The dispatch ran on November 10th.

00:07:22.256 --> 00:07:42.380
<v Riley>West Fraser is assessing a possible new sawmill near Inverness Airport in Scotland. According to The Inverness Courier, the company has finished public consultation ahead of a planning application expected before the end of 2026. West Fraser told Canadian Forest Industries it is still in the assessment phase and has made no decision.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.404
<v Casey>Consultation done, decision still open.

00:07:46.164 --> 00:08:19.898
<v Casey>U.S. feedlots placed 1.891 million cattle in October, down 7 per cent from a year earlier and the lowest October total in records going back to 1996, according to the U.S. Department of Agriculture's November 20th report. Analysts expected placements at 96.5 per cent of last year's level, and the actual figure was 92.7 per cent. The Hales Cattle Letter warned that continued heifer retention could cut marketings further. Oklahoma State University's Darrell Peel said the cumulative effect of the last three reports is something to watch closely.

00:08:20.348 --> 00:08:26.236
<v Riley>Heifer retention means producers keep young females for breeding instead of selling them, which tightens supply now.

00:08:27.975 --> 00:09:01.207
<v Riley>Sweden's 2026 grain harvest came in below last year's record but closer to normal, with spring barley and oats above normal in Mälardalen and western Sweden. Lantmännen's grain chief, Per Germundsson, called the results uneven across crops and regions. Official statistics show fertilizer and soil-improver prices up 14.6 per cent, and energy and fuel up 27.8 per cent, from September 2025 to September 2026. An unsettled fertilizer market is making the 2027 season uncertain.

00:09:01.657 --> 00:09:06.145
<v Casey>Good crop, dearer inputs, and the fertilizer bill for 2027 still unknown.

00:09:07.907 --> 00:09:38.361
<v Casey>Poland's agriculture ministry has asked the competition regulator, UOKiK, to investigate propane prices and supply after shortages and high costs disrupted corn drying at harvest. Temporary rules let LPG truck drivers work up to 11 hours a day instead of nine, from November 6th to December 6th. Some grain collection points have stopped accepting wet corn, and others have raised moisture deductions, according to farmers' chambers. The ministry hasn't announced drying subsidies, a price reduction, or guaranteed deliveries.

00:09:38.811 --> 00:09:43.219
<v Riley>Higher moisture deductions work as a price cut that never shows up on a price list.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:10:19.642
<v Riley>Farmers in Poland, Sweden, and Senegal are testing soil, water, and crop measures as drought, flooding, and extreme heat strain farming. A Polish report says raising soil-water retention across farmland by 20 millimetres could store 2.9 billion cubic metres of water. Poland spent more than 10 billion zloty on drought relief over the past decade. A Swedish research project is assessing a system that stores water in dry periods and releases it during floods. In Senegal, farmers report hotter planting seasons and fewer hours in the fields.

00:10:21.156 --> 00:10:51.682
<v Casey>Four farm profiles from Poland and Romania show small producers earning from saffron, pumpkin-seed processing, melons, and direct vegetable sales. Dorota Kaliszewska is testing saffron in the Lublin region. Filip and Magda Partyka run a 14-hectare vegetable farm, and their weekly boxes usually hold 10 to 13 items, with about 70 per cent of customers coming back the following year. Robert Zamisnicu, who grows vegetables and melons on 40 hectares in Romania, lost about half his crop to a hailstorm in early August.

00:10:52.132 --> 00:10:57.100
<v Riley>Seventy per cent returning customers is a retention number most farms would sign for.

00:10:58.919 --> 00:11:35.767
<v Riley>Algeria's Adrar province is building a $3.5 billion dairy and crop complex, planned for 270,000 dairy cows and up to 100,000 tonnes of milk powder a year, across about 117,000 hectares. Qatar's Baladna is developing it with Algeria's National Investment Fund. Production is scheduled to start in late 2027. Temperatures in the region top 40 degrees for more than 130 days a year, and the project needs continuous power and water. Baladna plans to bring in 30,000 Holstein cows from the United States.

00:11:36.217 --> 00:11:41.089
<v Casey>Forty-plus degrees for over 130 days a year, and every one of those cows needs water.

00:11:41.539 --> 00:11:47.931
<v Casey>We'll come back to who keeps the margin when a family stays put. First, a group working closer to the dinner table.

00:11:49.331 --> 00:12:18.971
<v Riley>The Williams Lake Food Policy Council works on local food security across the Cariboo, through community gardens, food recovery, and farm-to-table initiatives. Those are the everyday pieces of getting food from a field to a plate, and the council puts its effort into all three. If you grow food, rescue food that would otherwise be thrown out, or just want to know where what you eat comes from, this is the group to know. Ask around in Williams Lake, and someone will point you their way.

00:12:19.636 --> 00:12:24.636
<v Casey>Food recovery is the unglamorous end of food security, and it's where a council like that earns its keep.

00:12:24.736 --> 00:12:27.488
<v Casey>Back to the working land, starting with the arithmetic.

00:12:28.888 --> 00:12:44.888
<v Riley>Polish sugar beet growers are looking at next season with per-hectare costs climbing against a price that doesn't move with them. That means a heavy yield just to break even. The break-even yield is the floor, and everything above it is what the family keeps.

00:12:45.457 --> 00:12:53.433
<v Casey>So when a family keeps working the same land through thin years, is staying a choice they made, or just what's left after the money has gone somewhere else?

00:12:53.724 --> 00:13:00.292
<v Riley>Those aren't opposites. A family that stays through a bad cycle is betting it can ride out the cost curve, and some of them do.

00:13:00.836 --> 00:13:13.124
<v Casey>Some. Małgorzata Gromadzka, Poland's deputy agriculture minister, says retailers can put producers and processors in a take-it-or-leave-it position. A minister said that about her own sector. No farmer had to complain first.

00:13:13.694 --> 00:13:29.702
<v Riley>And the same forum is calling for longer-term contracts. Grupa Żywiec's corporate affairs director says buying local barley keeps the value where the beer is made. A buyer who commits for years gives a grower a planning horizon, and planning is what makes staying pay.

00:13:30.229 --> 00:13:37.021
<v Casey>Planning for whom? A long contract protects the buyer's supply. Nothing in it moves the grower's price when input costs move.

00:13:37.575 --> 00:14:03.343
<v Riley>The terms decide who it serves, so growers have to be at the table when they're written. The rules already give them something to push with. Poland's 2016 act on unfair use of contractual advantage in agri-food trade is enforced by UOKiK. UOKiK has charged buyers over contract terms that failed to excuse suppliers when weather caused non-performance. A regulator moved weather risk back off the farmer, in writing.

00:14:03.977 --> 00:14:23.409
<v Casey>In those cases, yes. It still doesn't touch the cost line. The new EU rules on cross-border unfair trading ban a list of abusive buyer practices and allow voluntary mediation through farmer organisations. A ban on abusive clauses doesn't fix a falling price. Input costs and price structure close a small farm, and no contract rule reaches either one.

00:14:23.976 --> 00:14:29.496
<v Riley>Then the rules set a floor. Competition does the fixing, and mediation is one channel into it.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:33.558
<v Casey>Voluntary means the buyer has to agree to sit down. Which buyer does that?

00:14:33.558 --> 00:14:35.942
<v Riley>The ones who lose volume when farmers walk.

00:14:36.452 --> 00:14:50.660
<v Casey>Ha. Some of them. UOKiK's dairy guidance covers exclusivity clauses, the ones that stop a farmer selling to anyone else. Where a farmer has one buyer in range, striking that clause changes nothing. The clause is gone, and the market is still one buyer long.

00:14:51.269 --> 00:15:04.685
<v Riley>I'll give you that one. A rule against exclusivity is worth little without a second buyer. But a second buyer can be a processor that builds its supply from a network of growers, and that's where some of the Polish cases are heading.

00:15:05.299 --> 00:15:21.907
<v Casey>Łukasz Popielewicz's pumpkin-seed company works that way. It relies on contract growers, cleaned about 5,000 tonnes last year, and is aiming for up to 9,000 this year. The company says the growers keep the margin. The processor sets the specification, the timing and the price.

00:15:22.473 --> 00:15:30.153
<v Riley>The processor also takes the processing risk. Cleaning to 99 per cent purity is capital the grower doesn't have to put up.

00:15:30.723 --> 00:15:40.259
<v Casey>That's the trade. The grower gets a buyer and gives up the say on the spec. Ask whether that trade was written down before the first planting or after the first bad harvest.

00:15:40.594 --> 00:15:44.034
<v Riley>Before planting, in a contract. That's the version to copy.

00:15:44.665 --> 00:16:06.705
<v Casey>Maybe. The squeeze doesn't hit every farm size the same way. Igor Płoski, a veterinarian and livestock farmer, says agricultural policy is squeezing out small farms while larger farms take on heavy debt as they expand. He wants direct payments aimed at active farmers. The forum in Poznań tomorrow, the 25th, takes on the pig sector too, where herds are shrinking and piglets are imported.

00:16:07.282 --> 00:16:23.898
<v Riley>Small farms can stay, though. The Partykas, the vegetable-box farm from the roundup, show how. Filip's advice is to plan costs, cash flow, and expected revenue before planting, and to stop choosing crops only because the land is there. They built the farm around the customer.

00:16:24.412 --> 00:16:31.044
<v Casey>It's one farm with a customer list. Ask how many small Polish holdings have one, and how many have a town close enough to sell to.

00:16:31.616 --> 00:16:37.496
<v Riley>Direct sales are the one channel a small farm controls outright. Staying long enough is how you build one.

00:16:38.068 --> 00:16:48.940
<v Casey>Or how you lose a crop while you wait. That hailstorm took half of Zamisnicu's season, and Kaliszewska lost some saffron bulbs to too much moisture in her first year. That's the cost of staying on one plot.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:54.908
<v Riley>Which is why those profiles put saffron and seed processing next to vegetables. Spread the bets.

00:16:55.223 --> 00:16:58.839
<v Casey>Spreading bets takes cash to survive the year none pay. Who has that?

00:16:59.238 --> 00:17:16.126
<v Riley>Tools, then. A survey previewed at PLUSAGRI in Warsaw on the 18th found that 53.9 per cent of participating Polish farmers say they use AI. Lantmännen in Sweden uses it in plant breeding and to tune fertilizer and water. The tools have left the trial plots.

00:17:16.736 --> 00:17:39.336
<v Casey>Who was asked? PLUSAGRI is a marketing-communications forum about how AI changes the relationship between brands and farmers. Its audience already goes to conferences. Polish national statistics put AI use among households at 39.8 per cent, and households aren't farms. A Warsaw University of Life Sciences study found about 66 per cent of farms still outside digital transformation.

00:17:39.921 --> 00:17:46.689
<v Riley>So 53.9 is an engaged sample. Engaged farmers keeping these tools still tells you the tools work well enough to keep.

00:17:47.374 --> 00:17:52.190
<v Casey>It tells you who gets tools first. Whoever can pay. Usually that's the bigger operation.

00:17:52.827 --> 00:18:09.643
<v Riley>Which is why data control matters more than the adoption number. Per Arfvidsson, Lantmännen's chief technology officer, says AI needs large volumes of quality-checked data, and farmers have to keep technical and legal control of their data. You can write that into a contract.

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:23.362
<v Casey>Cropin's founder says the whole supply chain needs an economic incentive to use AI. Cropin mainly sells its models to businesses and governments. So the paying customer is the business. Whose data trains the model, and who gets the output?

00:18:23.667 --> 00:18:34.731
<v Riley>That one has a contract answer too. Asseco Poland's Artur Wiza says digital investment should be judged by whether it saves labour and input costs. A farm can run that test on its own books.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:38.232
<v Casey>A farm can run that test. A vendor will run it on a slide.

00:18:38.921 --> 00:18:53.249
<v Riley>Climate is where the cost can't be passed to a buyer. Put the soil-water figure from the roundup next to the drought relief bill. Two point nine billion cubic metres of storage on one side, more than 10 billion zloty spent after the fact on the other.

00:18:53.887 --> 00:19:08.591
<v Casey>Relief arrives after the crop is gone. Soil work happens on the family's own land, in the family's own hours, before anyone pays for it. In Senegal, Abdoulaye Sognane says he can't work from one to five in the afternoon heat anymore. Nobody pays him for those hours.

00:19:09.236 --> 00:19:30.644
<v Riley>The adaptation is happening in place, though. Sebastian Bogucki, a farmer in Poland's West Pomeranian region, says crop residues should cover the soil to cut evaporation once its structure improves. Soil scientist Bartłomiej Glina says to assess structure, compaction, and water storage before relying on irrigation. A farm can make those moves this season.

00:19:31.295 --> 00:19:37.155
<v Casey>Cheap in cash. The family pays the learning curve in time, and time is what the margin has already squeezed.

00:19:37.155 --> 00:19:39.691
<v Riley>Which a farm can carry if the margin holds.

00:19:40.332 --> 00:20:18.978
<v Casey>And when it doesn't, there's the exit. Two Australian properties show the sharp version. Rushy Lagoon in Tasmania, about 22,000 hectares, sold for $142 million to a trust managed by Gresham House, a UK forestry asset manager. Australia's Clean Energy Finance Corporation put in $69 million, there was another $8.8 million in Australian federal plantation grants, and Gresham House and Aviva put in $73 million. Australian Treasurer Jim Chalmers approved it in July. Cobungra Station in Victoria, about 31,000 hectares that ran cattle for more than 170 years, went to the carbon fund Silva Capital for $50.3 million.

00:20:19.517 --> 00:20:37.261
<v Riley>A carbon buyer pays for land a cattle operation can't turn into cash on its own, and cattle margins are thin. At Cobungra, Raf Wood plans to keep grazing alongside native tree planting and put about $20 million into redevelopment. Tens of millions going into the land is the case for these sales.

00:20:37.787 --> 00:21:02.843
<v Casey>Grazing is in the owner's plan. Is it in an obligation anyone can enforce? And look at who's behind Silva's fund: BHP, Rio Tinto, Qantas, and Japan's JERA, whose $25 million is what triggered Australia's foreign investment review. The approval came through in August. Darren Chester, the shadow agriculture minister, wants Labor to stop selling productive farmland for foreign-backed carbon projects. He's the opposition, so weigh it as a political line.

00:21:03.528 --> 00:21:14.184
<v Riley>Chalmers called the Rushy Lagoon approval a rigorous process that would create about 190 jobs. Public money went in on purpose, to bring the private money with it.

00:21:14.729 --> 00:21:23.457
<v Casey>The jobs come with a pine plantation. TasFarmers called it a disgraceful outcome for Tasmania and for food security. Both of those are said about the same farm.

00:21:23.637 --> 00:21:24.733
<v Riley>Keep both in the record.

00:21:25.335 --> 00:21:33.423
<v Casey>Then price the downside. What happens to the cattle if the carbon price falls? The plantation stays in the ground. The herd doesn't rebuild itself.

00:21:33.696 --> 00:21:37.312
<v Riley>Then that's the clause to fight for, before the sale closes.

00:21:37.492 --> 00:21:37.972
<v Casey>Yes.

00:21:38.549 --> 00:21:47.013
<v Riley>The cattle side asks the same question from the other direction. Those record-low feedlot placements are partly families holding heifers back.

00:21:47.558 --> 00:21:58.806
<v Casey>Holding heifers back is staying in a different form. A rancher who keeps the breeding stock is betting the price holds long enough to pay for the cows they didn't sell. No cash this year for a bigger herd later.

00:21:59.389 --> 00:22:05.557
<v Riley>And only a family with savings can make that bet. The family without them takes the carbon fund's offer.

00:22:06.147 --> 00:22:11.531
<v Casey>Or a family that could hold on, and chose the price. From outside, a sale looks the same either way.

00:22:12.073 --> 00:22:20.921
<v Riley>The Cariboo version is the one I'd want in front of a ranch family. Their land stays Cariboo land whatever the price. The buyer decides what it's for.

00:22:21.535 --> 00:22:36.847
<v Casey>Which brings us home to Clinton. Earlier this fall, on October 13th, we took on foreign land ownership and Cariboo ranchers, and that was about who buys the land. The Pear Lake fire is about who carries the loss. A community forest holds the tenure. It doesn't hold the fire.

00:22:37.369 --> 00:22:55.361
<v Riley>Local ownership still decides the response. The forest chooses what gets salvaged, in what order, and who does the work. It has used $512,000 in funding to recover about 32,000 cubic metres of low-quality, residual wood. A distant owner wouldn't bother with wood that grade.

00:22:55.877 --> 00:23:07.717
<v Casey>Thirty-two thousand cubic metres off forty thousand burned hectares. Law himself says there's no way on earth they can salvage it all. A good plan can still lose money if buyers aren't there when the logs are.

00:23:08.237 --> 00:23:19.565
<v Riley>Community ownership doesn't guarantee the margin. It guarantees the people deciding live with the results, and the board has a permanent stake in what the next twenty years look like there.

00:23:20.179 --> 00:23:30.099
<v Casey>The savings, the options, whether a family could sell and walk away. That's what decides whether staying is courage or no way out, and it's the number no one outside the family sees.

00:23:30.771 --> 00:23:45.491
<v Riley>So where does the value of staying get written down? In a long contract, in local ownership of the land and the data, or in the price a family can sell for. I'd put it in the terms, and the Clinton forest shows local hands can make those calls.

00:23:46.165 --> 00:24:01.805
<v Casey>And if the grower can't walk away, the terms get signed before anyone reads them. What still isn't proven is whether a family with no savings can refuse anything. The Polish cases say the contract is the lever. Rushy Lagoon says the lever gets pulled before the family is in the room.

00:24:02.353 --> 00:24:19.681
<v Riley>Then we leave it there: the rules, the contract, and the savings, with the savings mattering most. If you farm, ranch, run a mill, or sit on a community forest board and know how that math works in your own operation, write to us at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Have a good Tuesday.

00:24:20.018 --> 00:24:23.986
<v Casey>And it's 2 degrees at Horsefly Lake, so mind the frost on the driveway.

00:24:24.586 --> 00:24:43.618
